FujiNet Go CoCo (desktop) -- what is done and what is next
==========================================================

The port follows fujinet-go-adam-desktop/PORTING.md, whose §12 milestone
order this file tracks. The derivation is

    coco-desktop = (adam-desktop - adam-android) + coco-android

with the Apple II port as the closer model (it already solved variable frame
geometry, analog axes and the ROM-embedding gate).


Done (this pass) -- M1, M2, M3
------------------------------

Build system
  * cmake/Dependencies.cmake -- coco_provide_dependency() with a new
    ARCHIVE/SHA256 branch, because XRoar ships a release tarball rather than a
    git repository. XRoar 1.11 and fujinet-firmware b9a27cfa1 are pinned.
  * cmake/StageXRoar.cmake -- stages src/ + portalib/ + top-config.h into
    core/xroar-generated, swaps in the lazy-connect becker.c, and runs
    tools/xroar/patch-staged-tree.py. Re-stages when the source, the patch
    script or the staging recipe changes; -DXROAR_RESTAGE=ON forces it.
  * tools/xroar/patch-staged-tree.py -- generates config.h (headless
    CoCo/Dragon feature set, host half resolved by the preprocessor) and
    registers our ui/ao modules in src/ui.c and src/ao.c under
    XROAR_DESKTOP_MODULES.
  * cmake/FujiNetRuntime.cmake, cmake/toolchains/mingw-w64.cmake.
  * WITH_COCO_ROMS gate + tools/xroar/embed-roms.py + the five ROM images.

Core
  * core/include/cocosession.h -- the frontend contract.
  * core/xroar/coco_host.{c,h} -- the Android host, with android/log.h
    replaced by a log-sink seam and a frame-rate sink added (the Android app
    ignored XRoar's notify_frame_rate; the desktop pacer needs it). Plus
    cocohost_release_all_keys() for focus loss.
  * core/xroar/desktop/{ui,ao,joystick}_desktop.c -- the three XRoar modules.
    ui_desktop.c uses VO_RENDER_FMT_ARGB8 (the explicit base format, never the
    VO_RENDER_FMT_ARGB32 alias), so every frontend paints XRGB8888 with no
    per-pixel conversion.
  * core/src/session.c -- lifecycle and the paced emulator loop. FujiNet is
    started BEFORE the emulator, which is the opposite of ADAM and Apple II
    and follows from FujiNet being the one that listens here.
  * core/src/input_map.c -- keysym -> XRoar hkbd scancode (USB HID usage
    codes), positional via a US-layout unshift; and the 0..65535 analog axis
    mapping with 32767 at centre.
  * core/src/paths.c -- materialises the embedded ROMs into the user's ROM
    directory, skipping names already present so an import always wins.
  * core/src/gamepad_sdl.c -- reworked for the CoCo's TWO joystick ports
    (first pad -> right, second -> left, explicit binding available).
  * core/src/{settings,audio_sdl,fujinet_runtime,compat.h,dynlib.h}.c -- from
    the Apple II port with the target-specific parts adjusted.

Frontend
  * frontends/gnome -- GTK4/libadwaita. Machine / Display / Artifact Colour as
    live radio actions in the menu (the Display submenu is rebuilt on a machine
    switch, which is what removes RGB on a CoCo 2), preferences over the shared
    settings store, media and ROM import, FujiNet config + log windows.

Tests
  * input_test -- the pure mapping functions.
  * boot_smoke -- writes the embedded ROMs to a scratch directory, starts the
    core headless, runs 120 frames and checks geometry, non-black pixels and
    that XRoar reported a frame rate. Skips (77) on WITH_COCO_ROMS=OFF.
  * drivewire_smoke -- the end-to-end one. Starts a real session with the
    in-process runtime and asserts (a) an OP_READ line in FujiNet's own log,
    which can only appear if bytes crossed the loopback socket in BOTH
    directions, and (b) that typing changes the screen. Both bugs above were
    found by writing it; neither was visible from the outside. Skips (77) on
    no ROMs, no libfujinet, or a Becker port someone else is holding -- and
    says which, because a leftover standalone fujinet-pc-coco is the single
    most confusing way for this to look broken.
  * session_test -- media/ROM import routing, ROM detection per machine, the
    menu enumerations, the live setters' clamping, and settings persistence
    across sessions. No emulator, so it runs in milliseconds.
  * dasm_test -- the 6809 disassembler: one named case per encoding that is
    easy to get wrong (page prefixes, every indexed postbyte shape, PCR
    resolving against the END of the instruction, the push/pull register
    lists), plus a sweep over all 65536 two-byte openings asserting only that
    every one decodes to a usable length, so a disassembly list can never get
    stuck.
  * no_embedded_roms.py -- registered per shipped binary when
    WITH_COCO_ROMS=OFF.

M4 is done: settings, media import, gamepad port assignment and the audio
block-size contract are all covered. coco_pad_for_port is split out of
gamepad_sdl.c so the two-port assignment rules can be tested with no hardware
attached; writing those assertions found that it would happily assign a pad to
a third joystick port the CoCo does not have.

FujiNet runtime (M3)
  * tools/fujinet/build-fujinet-desktop.sh -- PC_TARGET=COCO, [BOIP] forced to
    127.0.0.1:65504. All eleven of the Apple II script's patch blocks are
    target-independent and applied unchanged; the COCO/DriveWire bus needs no
    transport transform, because the Becker port is already plain DriveWire
    over TCP. Built clean on the first attempt against fujinet-pc-coco
    b9a27cfa1, exporting all six ABI symbols.
  * tools/fujinet/support/fujinet_desktop_entry.cpp -- taken from the Apple II
    *working tree* rather than its last commit, for the stop-with-timeout fix
    (a device blocked on a socket call can miss the shutdown flag; detaching
    after 5s beats hanging the host process on exit).
  * Web admin on 64002, not the Android app's 8002: a standalone
    fujinet-pc-coco uses 8002. ADAM has 65214 and Apple II 64001, so all three
    desktop apps plus a standalone build can run at once.
  * fujinet_runtime.c sets FUJINET_RUNTIME_ROOT before starting the runtime.
    Two firmware patches root themselves from it and fall back to a relative
    path when unset -- which fails quietly (the CA store loads empty and every
    https:// fetch starts looking like a network fault). The Apple II port
    does not set it either; worth telling them.

  * fujinet_wait_for_becker() -- start is NOT enough. main_setup() returns as
    soon as the devices exist, but the DriveWire bus then logs "BoIPChannel:
    No WiFi!" and suspends itself for a FULL SECOND before listening. HDB-DOS
    boots in well under that, asks for its boot sector, gets nothing, and
    hangs on the banner with no OK prompt -- which looks exactly like a
    working emulator until you try to type. The session now probes the port
    until it accepts before starting the machine.
  * paths_init() resolves config/data/rom directories to absolute paths. The
    FujiNet entry wrapper chdir()s into its runtime root, and on this target
    FujiNet starts FIRST, so by the time XRoar resolves -rompath the CWD has
    moved and a relative path silently finds nothing: "(unpopulated)" ROM
    slots and a blank screen, with the files right there on disk.

Verified by hand
  * The CoCo 3 boots to its banner; the vsync phase lock engages at 60/60
    frames on vsync, 0 behind (COCO_PACE_LOG=1).
  * HDB-DOS reaches FujiNet's CONFIG over the Becker port, against both a
    standalone fujinet-pc-coco and the in-process runtime.
  * Clone-and-build both ways (M2): a fresh tree with no third_party/, and a
    `git archive` export with no git metadata at all. Both download the pinned
    XRoar tarball, verify its SHA256, stage it and test green.
  * Both ROM configurations: ctest green either way, and no_embedded_roms
    fails loudly on a WITH_COCO_ROMS=ON binary, as it should.


Next
----


M6 (in progress) The 6809 disassembler is done -- core/debugger/m6809dasm.c,
    with the opcode tables extracted mechanically from XRoar's
    mc6809_trace.c rather than retyped, so they cannot drift by a typo.
    Still open:
      * The engine. core/debugger/debugger.c is still a placeholder. Build it
        on XRoar's struct debug_cpu (src/debug_cpu.h: instruction_hook /
        instruction_posthook / get_pc / set_pc) and src/breakpoint.c rather
        than inventing a stepped core path.
      * core/debugger/cocovideo.c -- VDG text/semigraphics/PMODE and the
        CoCo 3 GIME modes, for the debugger's video pane.
      * The windows, in both frontends (placeholders that say so today).
      * 6309 opcodes. The API takes no CPU parameter yet; hd6309_trace.c has
        the same table shape, so the same extraction works when a machine
        config that selects a 6309 is offered.

M7  CI: linux, then macOS and Windows, then flatpak.

M8  Icons, desktop entries, AppStream metainfo, screenshots.
    (The GNOME .desktop and .metainfo.xml exist; the icon art does not, so
    data/icons is still empty and the top-level install() rules for it have
    nothing to install yet.)

M9  Packaging + release job.

M10 macOS and Windows frontends, remaining debugger windows, polish.


Known issues / things to remember
---------------------------------

* src/path.c:194 hardcodes ':' as the rompath separator. On Windows that
  collides with a drive letter. Deal with it at M10 (or send a fix upstream).
* MinGW-w64 has no regex.h and portalib/sdsx.c needs one, reached from
  src/path.c and src/xconfig.c. config.h already defines HAVE_TRE on _WIN32
  and core/CMakeLists.txt links systre/tre; the Windows toolchain has to
  provide them (see cmake/toolchains/mingw-w64.cmake).
* Restarting a session calls xroar_shutdown() and then xroar_init() again.
  The Android app never does this (it kills the process), so it is untested.
  If it turns out XRoar cannot be re-initialised in one process, the fix is to
  make the session's restart path rebuild the machine in place instead --
  cocohost_set_machine() already does exactly that without re-init.
* prefs.c casts g_free to GClosureNotify and gcc warns. Carried over from the
  Apple II port, where it warns identically; it is a GLib API shape, not a
  bug here.
